(Bug #74's other half. Every sentence the fix now refuses has a documented
 way to say what it meant, and both ways answer correctly. The refused
 spellings are tests/compile_fail/182-198; this is what those programs
 become, and what they print is the answer their authors were after.)

a list called counts is [10, 20, 30].
a map called ages is {"ada": 36, "bea": 41}.

(`a text called t is counts's length.` segfaulted on the length read as a
 pointer. Either declare the type the property has...)
a number called tally is counts's length.
print tally.
(...or convert it, the way the diagnostic's help line spells it.)
a text called spelled is counts's length as text.
print spelled.

(`a list called L is counts's length.` walked a list header at address 3.)
a number called 'how many' is counts's length.
print 'how many'.

(`a text called t is ledger's capacity.` faulted the same way.)
a buffer called ledger is 16 bytes in size.
a number called room is ledger's capacity.
print room.
a text called 'room spelled' is ledger's capacity as text.
print 'room spelled'.

(`a number called n is counts's empty.` put a boolean in a number's slot. It
 happened to print 0, which is why nobody noticed; the boolean is what the
 manual's table promises, and the cast is what says otherwise out loud.)
a boolean called drained is counts's empty.
print drained.
a number called 'drained as a number' is counts's empty as a number.
print 'drained as a number'.

(`a boolean called done is counts's length.` printed 3 out of a boolean.)
a boolean called 'any at all' is counts's empty.
print 'any at all'.

(`a number called what is gauge's type.` printed the literal's address.
 `type` is text.)
a number called gauge is 4.
a text called described is gauge's type.
print described.

(`a text called t is ages's keys.` printed nothing at all - a list header
 read as a string. Keys are a list.)
a list called who is ages's keys.
print who.

(`a text called t is gauge's even.` faulted on address 1.)
a boolean called halves is gauge's even.
print halves.
a text called 'halves spelled' is gauge's even as text.
print 'halves spelled'.

(The three sites past the declaration - an argument, a return and a `Set` -
 each written so the types agree.)
To 'shout' with a text called word. Return a text, word.
To 'count' with a list called items. Return a number, items's length.

print 'shout' of counts's length as text.
print 'count' of counts.

a text called label is "start".
Set label to counts's length as text.
print label.
